{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01646ADRPK9o8SugahJ7poXT","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the results:\n\n**APC 0:**\n- Round 0: 179ms (before) → 178ms (after) = -1ms (negligible, as expected with only 99 AIRs)\n- STARK excl trace: 2165ms (before) → 2178ms (after) = +13ms (within noise)\n\n**APC 100:**\n- Round 0: 465ms (before) → 466ms (after) = +1ms (no change)\n- STARK excl trace: 2163ms (before) → 2160ms (after) = -3ms (within noise)\n\n\n**APC 300:**\n- Round 0: 667ms (before) → 657ms (after) = -10ms (only 1.5% improvement)\n- STARK excl trace: 2468ms (before) → 2455ms (after) = -13ms (0.5% improvement)\n\nThe APC 300 results are underwhelming—just a 10ms gain on Round 0, nowhere near the expected 172-200ms reduction. This suggests the CPU post-processing I thought was the bottleneck is either already optimized or not where the time is actually being spent.\n\nLet me reconsider what's happening. The 200ms gap I calculated earlier came from subtracting GPU kernel time from the total, but that doesn't necessarily mean it's all CPU work—it could be kernel launch overhead, D2H transfer time, or other hidden costs. And since the D2H copies still run sequentially in Phase 1, the actual per-AIR CPU work like transpose and iDFT might be much smaller than I estimated.\n\nThe 10ms improvement at APC 300 is clearly below the 66ms threshold, so this optimization needs to be rolled back. I'll finalize the report and revert the changes.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2690,"cache_read_input_tokens":61035,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2690},"output_tokens":11,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"24111524-5945-406a-b911-d18148cb28ce","uuid":"b3008805